# ComposeUX on the Spotify Car Thing [SPEC:CXC-CARTHING]

> A discontinued $-cheap gadget with a 4" touchscreen, a clicky rotary encoder, and four momentary buttons — reflashed with the community DeskThing firmware — is a near-perfect ComposeUX dogfood surface: it exercises the **headed-display AND the input-HAL story in a single off-the-shelf device**, and it is the same input model as the custom PCBs, bought instead of built.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-CARTHING-INTENT] A Bought Surface That Proves the Input-HAL Story
- device: the Spotify Car Thing (discontinued) — ~800×480 LCD, a **rotary encoder with press**, **4 top momentary buttons** + a back button, ARM/Linux internals. The **DeskThing** community project reflashes it into a useful desk gadget that runs a webview + apps and exposes the hardware inputs.
- why it matters: most Compose surfaces are display-only (CYD) or input-only (the actuator PCBs). The Car Thing is BOTH at once — a headed ComposeUX display whose bezel carries real physical controls. A rotary encoder bound to a Compose selector/dial widget is a genuinely lovely interaction, and it validates that a display and its input-HAL peripherals compose into one device config ([CXC-HAL-DEVICE]).
- convergence: its encoder + switches are the SAME Tier-1 input-HAL shape as the `button_matrix_4x4` / gauge PCBs ([CXC-HAL-ACTUATE], [CXC-HAL-TIERS]) — but off-the-shelf and polished. It de-risks the input-HAL model on hardware nobody had to solder.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CARTHING]

---

## 2. The Integration Model

### [CXC-CARTHING-MODEL] App-First, Fork-Only-If-It-Fights-You (David 2026-07-10)
- **Option 1 — ComposeUX runner as a DeskThing app (RECOMMENDED, do this first)**: ComposeUX already has a web render target; DeskThing runs a webview + exposes hardware events via its app API. So a thin DeskThing app = point a webview at a ComposeUX surface + map the encoder/buttons to ComposeBus namespaces. Low effort; keeps DeskThing's maintained plumbing (flashing, updates, input drivers) instead of owning it.
- **Option 2 — fork DeskThing into a pure ComposeUX surface (DEFERRED)**: replace the app sandbox with a dedicated boot-to-ComposeUX runtime. Only becomes right if the app sandbox constrains us — latency, exclusive fullscreen, direct-framebuffer, boot-to-Compose. Don't pay that cost on spec; let the app version reveal whether it's needed.
- decision: start Option 1. Promote to Option 2 only on a concrete constraint from the app build.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CARTHING]

### [CXC-CARTHING-INPUTS] Encoder + Switches Are Tier-1 Input HAL
- the physical controls are input sensors in the HAL model ([CXC-HAL-TIERS] Tier 1): the **rotary encoder → a delta namespace** (e.g. `nav/rotate`), **encoder press → `nav/select`**, **back button → `nav/back`**, the **4 top buttons → `nav/1..4`** (or semantic names). DeskThing surfaces these events; the app forwards them to ComposeBus.
- bus-native symmetry: because inputs publish to namespaces, an Intelligence can "turn the knob" or "press select" identically to a human finger — the same property the touch/gesture engine already has ([CXC-RM-GESTURE]).
- widget fit: bind `nav/rotate` to a Compose selector/dial/list-cursor widget; the encoder scrubs a value or moves focus, the press commits. This is the reference interaction for rotary input across the whole widget set.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CARTHING]

---

## 3. Open Decisions (before build)

### [CXC-CARTHING-OPEN] What Needs Deciding
- **DeskThing app API surface**: exactly how it exposes the encoder/button events and hosts a webview (poll vs. event stream; fullscreen/kiosk guarantees). Determines the thin-app shape and whether Option 2 is ever forced.
- **input namespaces**: the canonical `nav/*` set (device-generic) vs. per-surface semantic names, and how a `.compose.yaml` declares which control drives which widget.
- **rendering path**: the ComposeUX web backend (Decision 1 outcome, [CXC-RM-D1]) served locally on-device vs. from a bus host; latency + offline behavior on the Car Thing's internals.
- **encoder semantics**: raw delta vs. accumulated position vs. detented steps; velocity for fast scrub.

---

## 4. Open-Source Framing

The Car Thing is a **reference surface** for the open-source release — alongside the WLED adaptor ([CXC-HAL-ACTUATE], [CXC-RM-WLED]), it proves "bring your existing/cheap hardware onto the bus without touching the core." A repurposed-gadget surface with real physical controls is a compelling, shareable demo and a template makers can copy for other DeskThing-class devices.

---

**Status**: CONCEPT CAPTURE (David 2026-07-10). Model settled — DeskThing-app-first, fork deferred; encoder/switches = Tier-1 input HAL → namespaces; a dual display+input reference surface. [CXC-CARTHING-OPEN] decisions need shaping before build-ready. Not on the critical path; a high-value dogfood/reference target that converges the headed-display and input-HAL stories.
